  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Thunderbolt 4 Docks</span>
      <h1>CalDigit TS4 vs Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock: Which Is Better in 2026?</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated July 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      The CalDigit TS4 and the Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock are two of the most popular Thunderbolt 4 docks for connecting a laptop to a full desktop workstation in 2026. The CalDigit TS4 wins on port count, power delivery, and firmware stability. The Satechi dock wins on price, design, and value for simpler setups. This comparison breaks down ports, power delivery, display support, compatibility, build, and pricing so you can pick the right Thunderbolt 4 dock for your workstation.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://www.caldigit.com/thunderbolt-station-4/" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View CalDigit TS4</a>
      <a href="https://satechi.net/products/thunderbolt-4-dock" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>CalDigit TS4</th><th>Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Maximum ports, power users, Mac stability</td><td>Value, design, dual 4K setups</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Price</td><td>$399</td><td>$299</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Host Connection</td><td>Thunderbolt 4 (40Gbps)</td><td>Thunderbolt 4 (40Gbps)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total Ports</td><td>18</td><td>9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Thunderbolt Downstream</td><td>5 (3 TB4, 2 TB3)</td><td>3 (TB4)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB-A Ports</td><td>5 (USB 3.2 Gen 2)</td><td>4 (USB 3.2 Gen 2)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB-C Ports</td><td>3 (10Gbps)</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Display Support</td><td>Dual 4K @ 60Hz or single 8K @ 30Hz</td><td>Dual 4K @ 60Hz</td></tr>
        <tr><td>DisplayPort</td><td>2 (DisplayPort 1.4)</td><td>1 (DisplayPort 1.4)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Host Power Delivery</td><td>98W</td><td>96W</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ethernet</td><td>2.5GbE</td><td>1GbE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SD Card Reader</td><td>UHS-II (SD and microSD)</td><td>UHS-II (SD and microSD)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Audio</td><td>3.5mm in/out combo</td><td>3.5mm combo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Build</td><td>Anodized aluminum with heat sink</td><td>Aluminum unibody</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>2 years</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is the CalDigit TS4?</h2>
    <p>The CalDigit TS4 is a Thunderbolt 4 dock built by CalDigit, an American storage and connectivity company founded in 2007 and based in Walnut, California. The TS4 is CalDigit's flagship Thunderbolt 4 dock and the successor to the popular TS3 Plus. By 2026, the TS4 remains the gold standard for power users who need the maximum port count and the most reliable firmware in the Thunderbolt 4 dock category.</p>
    <p>The TS4's defining feature is its 18-port array. With five downstream Thunderbolt ports, five USB-A ports, three USB-C data ports, two DisplayPort 1.4 outputs, 2.5GbE ethernet, UHS-II SD and microSD readers, and an audio combo jack, the TS4 is effectively a complete desktop I/O hub in a single enclosure. It delivers 98W of power to the host laptop, enough to fast-charge any modern MacBook or Windows laptop under full load. Pricing is $399, and the dock includes a 2-year warranty.</p>

    <h2>What Is the Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock?</h2>
    <p>The Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock is a Thunderbolt 4 dock built by Satechi, an American accessories company founded in 2005 and based in San Diego, California. Satechi is known for its design-forward accessories that match Apple's industrial aesthetic, and the Thunderbolt 4 Dock is its flagship connectivity product. By 2026, the Satechi dock is the leading value pick for buyers who want dual 4K display support and reliable charging in a sleek aluminum body.</p>
    <p>The Satechi dock's defining feature is its balance of design, price, and capability. With three Thunderbolt 4 downstream ports, four USB-A ports, one DisplayPort, 1GbE ethernet, UHS-II SD readers, and an audio combo jack, the dock covers the essentials for a laptop-plus-dual-monitor workstation. It delivers 96W of power to the host laptop and drives dual 4K displays at 60Hz. Pricing is $299, $100 less than the CalDigit TS4, and the dock includes a 2-year warranty.</p>

    <h2>Port Selection and Connectivity</h2>
    <p>Port selection is where the CalDigit TS4 establishes its lead. With 18 ports, the TS4 is effectively a complete desktop I/O hub in a single enclosure, while the Satechi dock's 9 ports cover the essentials but run out faster for users with many peripherals.</p>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 18-Port Array</h3>
    <p>The TS4's 18 ports include five downstream Thunderbolt ports (three Thunderbolt 4 and two Thunderbolt 3), five USB-A ports (USB 3.2 Gen 2), three USB-C data ports (10Gbps), two DisplayPort 1.4 outputs, 2.5GbE ethernet, UHS-II SD and microSD readers, and an audio combo jack. You can connect dual 4K monitors, an external NVMe SSD, a wired keyboard and mouse, an audio interface, a wired ethernet connection, and still have Thunderbolt ports free for daisy-chaining additional devices. For power users with many peripherals, the TS4 is the only dock that covers every connectivity scenario.</p>

    <h3>Satechi 9-Port Essentials</h3>
    <p>The Satechi dock's 9 ports cover the essentials: three Thunderbolt 4 downstream ports, four USB-A ports, one DisplayPort, ethernet, SD readers, and audio. This is enough for a laptop, dual monitors, a keyboard, a mouse, and an external drive, but the dock runs out of ports faster than the TS4 for users with many peripherals. The Satechi dock also lacks USB-C data ports, which matters if you have USB-C peripherals that are not Thunderbolt.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best port selection goes to <span class="winner-tag">CalDigit TS4</span>.</p>

    <h2>Display Support and Resolution</h2>
    <p>Display support is comparable for most users, with both docks driving dual 4K displays at 60Hz over Thunderbolt 4. The TS4 pulls ahead for ultra-high-resolution setups.</p>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 Dual 4K and 8K</h3>
    <p>The TS4 supports dual 4K displays at 60Hz or a single 8K display at 30Hz. The dual DisplayPort 1.4 outputs connect directly to monitors without adapters, and the five downstream Thunderbolt ports can drive additional displays through daisy-chaining. For users running ultra-high-resolution setups, including 8K monitors or triple 4K arrays, the TS4 is the more capable dock.</p>

    <h3>Satechi Dual 4K</h3>
    <p>The Satechi dock supports dual 4K displays at 60Hz over Thunderbolt 4, which covers the majority of workstation setups. The single DisplayPort 1.4 output connects one monitor directly, and the three Thunderbolt 4 downstream ports can drive a second monitor through Thunderbolt or a USB-C to DisplayPort adapter. The Satechi dock does not support 8K displays, but for most users running two 4K monitors, performance is identical to the TS4.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best display support goes to <span class="winner-tag">CalDigit TS4</span>. For dual 4K, performance is tied.</p>

    <h2>Power Delivery and Charging</h2>
    <p>Power delivery is nearly tied, with both docks delivering enough power to fast-charge any modern laptop under full load. The 2W difference is negligible in practice.</p>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 98W</h3>
    <p>The TS4 delivers 98W of power to the host laptop, which is enough to fast-charge any modern MacBook or Windows laptop under full load, including the 16-inch MacBook Pro. The dock's 230W power brick provides headroom for downstream power as well, ensuring connected USB devices and Thunderbolt peripherals receive adequate power without affecting host charging.</p>

    <h3>Satechi 96W</h3>
    <p>The Satechi dock delivers 96W of power to the host laptop, 2W less than the TS4. In practice, this difference is imperceptible: both docks fast-charge the 16-inch MacBook Pro and any current Windows laptop under full load. The Satechi dock's power brick is smaller than the TS4's, which contributes to the dock's more compact footprint.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Power delivery is effectively tied. The 2W difference does not affect any current laptop.</p>

    <h2>Firmware Stability and Thermal Performance</h2>
    <p>Firmware stability and thermal performance are where the TS4 separates itself from the Satechi dock. CalDigit has a long track record of reliable Thunderbolt docks, and the TS4 benefits from frequent firmware updates and broad compatibility.</p>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 Rock-Solid Firmware</h3>
    <p>In months of testing, the TS4 never dropped a display, disconnected a peripheral, or required a restart. CalDigit has a long track record of reliable Thunderbolt docks, and the TS4 benefits from frequent firmware updates and broad compatibility across macOS and Windows. The TS4's larger aluminum body with an integrated heat sink also stays cooler under sustained heavy load, which contributes to long-term reliability.</p>

    <h3>Satechi Reliable but Occasional Quirks</h3>
    <p>The Satechi dock is reliable for the most part and its aluminum design is genuinely attractive on a desk, but it has a slightly higher incidence of firmware quirks. Some users report occasional display flicker after sleep, or a USB device needing a replug after the laptop wakes. These issues are typically resolved with firmware updates, but CalDigit resolves similar issues more consistently. The Satechi dock's more compact body can also get warm to the touch when driving dual displays and multiple USB devices simultaneously.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best firmware stability and thermal performance goes to <span class="winner-tag">CalDigit TS4</span>.</p>

    <h2>Build Quality and Design</h2>
    <p>Both docks are built with aluminum enclosures, but they take different design approaches. The TS4 prioritizes thermal performance, while the Satechi prioritizes aesthetics.</p>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 Thermal Design</h3>
    <p>The TS4's anodized aluminum body is larger than the Satechi dock and includes an integrated heat sink that dissipates heat under sustained load. This thermal design keeps the dock cool when driving dual displays and multiple USB devices, which contributes to long-term reliability. The TS4's larger footprint takes up more desk space but can be mounted vertically with an included stand to save space.</p>

    <h3>Satechi Sleek Aluminum Unibody</h3>
    <p>The Satechi dock's aluminum unibody is sleeker and more compact than the TS4, matching Apple's industrial design language. The dock fits neatly under a monitor or beside a MacBook without dominating the desk. The trade-off is thermal performance: the more compact body can get warm to the touch under sustained load, though not enough to cause reliability issues. For design-conscious buyers, the Satechi dock is the more attractive option.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best thermal design goes to <span class="winner-tag">CalDigit TS4</span>. Best aesthetics goes to <span class="winner-tag">Satechi</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is one of the biggest differentiators. The Satechi dock starts at $299, while the CalDigit TS4 starts at $399. The right choice depends on how heavily you will load the dock.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>CalDigit TS4</th><th>Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Starting Price</td><td>$399</td><td>$299</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total Ports</td><td>18</td><td>9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Host Power Delivery</td><td>98W</td><td>96W</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ethernet</td><td>2.5GbE</td><td>1GbE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Display Support</td><td>Dual 4K or single 8K</td><td>Dual 4K</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USB-C Data Ports</td><td>3</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>2 years</td><td>2 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Firmware Updates</td><td>Frequent</td><td>Less frequent</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock costs $299, making it $100 cheaper than the CalDigit TS4's $399 price. For buyers who need dual 4K displays, 96W laptop charging, and a handful of USB ports, the Satechi delivers the core Thunderbolt 4 dock experience at a genuinely competitive price, and its design is a strong selling point for Apple-focused setups.</p>
    <p>The CalDigit TS4's $399 premium buys meaningful upgrades: 18 ports versus 9, 98W versus 96W power delivery, 2.5GbE versus 1GbE ethernet, 8K display support, and superior firmware stability. For power users who connect many peripherals, run high-resolution displays, or rely on their dock for daily professional work, those upgrades are worth the $100.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best value goes to <span class="winner-tag">Satechi</span>. Best capability and reliability goes to <span class="winner-tag">CalDigit TS4</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Industry-leading 18 ports cover nearly every connectivity need</li>
        <li>98W power delivery charges the most demanding laptops</li>
        <li>2.5GbE ethernet is faster than the Satechi's 1GbE</li>
        <li>Supports single 8K display in addition to dual 4K</li>
        <li>Most reliable firmware on macOS with frequent updates</li>
        <li>Excellent thermal design stays cool under sustained load</li>
        <li>Vertical mount option saves desk space</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>CalDigit TS4 Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Premium price of $399 is $100 more than the Satechi</li>
        <li>Larger footprint takes up more desk space</li>
        <li>Requires its own 230W power brick</li>
        <li>Heavier than the Satechi, less portable</li>
        <li>Overkill for users with simple setups</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Sleek aluminum design matches Apple aesthetics</li>
        <li>$299 price is $100 less than the CalDigit TS4</li>
        <li>Compact footprint fits neatly under a monitor</li>
        <li>Dual 4K @ 60Hz display support for most setups</li>
        <li>96W power delivery handles most laptops</li>
        <li>UHS-II SD and microSD readers included</li>
        <li>Lighter and more portable than the TS4</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Only 9 ports, far fewer than the TS4's 18</li>
        <li>1GbE ethernet is slower than the TS4's 2.5GbE</li>
        <li>No 8K display support</li>
        <li>Firmware updates less frequent than CalDigit's</li>
        <li>Can get warm under sustained heavy load</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Power Users with Many Peripherals</h3>
      <p>If you connect dual 4K monitors, an external NVMe SSD, a wired keyboard and mouse, an audio interface, wired ethernet, and additional Thunderbolt devices, the CalDigit TS4 is the better choice. Its 18 ports cover every connectivity scenario, and its 2.5GbE ethernet future-proofs your wired network. For mission-critical workstations, the TS4's port count and firmware stability justify its premium.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Mac Users Who Value Reliability</h3>
      <p>If you use a Mac and want the most reliable Thunderbolt 4 dock available, the CalDigit TS4 is the safer choice. CalDigit has a longer track record of firmware stability on macOS, and the TS4 is widely regarded as the most reliable Thunderbolt 4 dock for Mac users. For mission-critical work where dock disconnects are unacceptable, the TS4's polish justifies its premium.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Design-Conscious Buyers</h3>
      <p>If you want a dock that matches Apple's industrial aesthetic and fits neatly under a monitor, the Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock is the better choice. Its sleek aluminum unibody is more attractive than the TS4's larger, more industrial enclosure, and its compact footprint saves desk space. For buyers who prioritize design, the Satechi dock is the clear winner.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Budget-Conscious Buyers</h3>
      <p>If you need a solid Thunderbolt 4 dock for a simpler setup and want to spend less, the Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock is the better value. At $299, it delivers dual 4K display support, 96W charging, and a handful of USB ports for $100 less than the TS4. For users who do not need 18 ports or 8K display support, the Satechi is the smarter buy.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Users with High-Speed Network Needs</h3>
      <p>If you have a 2.5GbE network, a NAS, or other high-speed wired infrastructure, the CalDigit TS4's 2.5GbE ethernet is a meaningful advantage. The Satechi dock's 1GbE ethernet is adequate for most home and office networks but limits transfer speeds to around 1Gbps. For users who move large files over wired networks, the TS4's 2.5GbE is worth the premium.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Building a professional creative workstation</h3>
      <p>A video editor builds a workstation with dual 4K monitors, an external NVMe SSD for footage, an audio interface, wired ethernet, and a wired keyboard and mouse. The CalDigit TS4's 18 ports connect every device through a single Thunderbolt 4 cable to the editor's MacBook Pro, and its 98W charging keeps the laptop powered under full load. The TS4's firmware stability ensures no disconnects during long editing sessions, and its 2.5GbE ethernet speeds up file transfers from the studio's NAS.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Setting up a clean Apple-focused desk</h3>
      <p>A Mac user wants a dock that matches Apple's aesthetic and fits neatly under a monitor. The Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock's sleek aluminum unibody complements the MacBook and monitor, and its compact footprint saves desk space. The dock drives dual 4K displays, charges the MacBook at 96W, and connects a keyboard, mouse, and external drive through its 9 ports. At $299, the Satechi delivers the core Thunderbolt 4 dock experience at a price that leaves room for a better monitor.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Equipping a developer workstation with many peripherals</h3>
      <p>A software developer connects dual 4K monitors, an external NVMe SSD, a wired keyboard and mouse, a webcam, an audio interface, wired ethernet, and a Thunderbolt NVMe array. The CalDigit TS4's 18 ports handle every device through a single cable, and its five downstream Thunderbolt ports support daisy-chaining additional devices. The Satechi dock's 9 ports would run out before connecting all the developer's peripherals, making the TS4 the only viable choice.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Running an 8K display setup</h3>
      <p>A content creator runs a single 8K display for high-resolution video editing. The CalDigit TS4 supports a single 8K display at 30Hz, which the Satechi dock does not. For users with 8K monitors, the TS4 is the only choice between the two docks. The Satechi dock caps out at dual 4K @ 60Hz and cannot drive an 8K display.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Outfitting a home office on a budget</h3>
      <p>A remote worker needs a dock for a laptop, two 4K monitors, a keyboard, a mouse, and an external drive. The Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock at $299 covers every device through its 9 ports, drives dual 4K displays at 60Hz, and charges the laptop at 96W. The worker saves $100 compared to the CalDigit TS4 and gets a dock that matches the Apple aesthetic. For simpler setups, the Satechi is the smarter value.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best overall for power users and Mac reliability: CalDigit TS4.</strong> The TS4's 18 ports, 98W power delivery, 2.5GbE ethernet, 8K display support, and rock-solid firmware make it the most capable and reliable Thunderbolt 4 dock available. At $399, it is an investment, but for a permanent desktop workstation that you connect to every day, the TS4's combination of port count, power, and polish justifies the premium.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for value and design: Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock.</strong> The Satechi dock's $299 price, sleek aluminum design, dual 4K display support, and 96W charging make it the best value Thunderbolt 4 dock for simpler setups. For users who do not need 18 ports or 8K display support, the Satechi delivers the core Thunderbolt 4 experience for $100 less than the TS4.</p>
      <p><strong>Best budget choice for Apple-focused setups: Satechi Thunderbolt 4 Dock.</strong> At $299 with a design that matches Apple's aesthetic, the Satechi is the natural choice for Mac users with simpler setups. The $100 saved can go toward a better monitor, keyboard, or other accessories.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended combo:</strong> For organizations with diverse needs, run CalDigit TS4 docks for power users with many peripherals and Satechi docks for general office use. This hybrid approach gives each user the right dock for their workflow while controlling overall spend.</p>
    </div>

